From 70c6c2b814ad26ba53cbb64b6dcf24dab2169b0a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SteveDeFacto <5148995+SteveDeFacto@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 06:04:53 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Chain authority: a host's chain reads through its local
 verifier, submissions through its own backend, nothing else

network/chain-authority.mjs: under the operator's explicit ENCLAVE_CHAIN_AUTHORITY=verifier + ENCLAVE_CHAIN_VERIFIER, a
viem transport that routes every method by name with the trust it actually has (METHODS):
  * the local verifier (Helios on its sequencer-signed head chain, the unsafe tier): eth_call/estimateGas executed over
    proven state; balance, code, storage and nonce proofs; blocks; receipts against the receipts root ("not found" is no
    evidence, mapped to null as a provider's null is, so callers decide by the proven nonce); logs proven for INCLUSION
    only, never completeness;
  * the operator's OWN backend (ENCLAVE_CHAIN_TX_BACKEND: a loopback or private IPv4 literal, never a name): submission,
    the pending-pool nonce and fee hints only; before each submission it is bound to the verifier's chain (chain id and
    the hash at the verifier's confirmed block) on a fresh head. A submission proves nothing; a verified receipt does.
    Without it nothing is submitted;
  * anything else is refused by name; an invalid selection refuses everything (refusingChainAuthority); no provider, no
    public default, no fallback. actionGate({what, submit}) is the freshness/binding check callers run before signing.
leaseRpcSet (client/ and network/ copies, kept identical): a client marked by this module (an in-process symbol, never
from configuration) is ONE authority - required 1, never mixed with RPC peers, never repeated into a quorum; RPC quorums
keep their exact rules. network/lease-gas-runtime.mjs: under the verifier the app-funded lease reads use it alone and the
configured RPCS are never contacted (kept accepted, so the reviewed lease-gas configuration stays byte-identical).
metal/guest/chain-verifier.mjs (the guest opt-in checker, dependency-free): optional txBackend under the same address rule
(checkTxBackendUrl; a parity test holds the two rules equal), and ENCLAVE_CHAIN_TX_BACKEND joins the names only this
channel supplies, set only with a usable verifier (a refusing verifier submits nothing).
